Our focus is to extract (from each audit report) sentences that indicate adverse remarks by the auditors. These adverse remarks provide crucial help in saying whether the associated FS might contain misinformation or not. We created several datasets from these two raw datasets (Table LABEL:tab:dataset\_summ).

* • Dataset D0D\_{0}: We used linguistic rules from [PAPV23] to automatically label each sentence from the dataset Da​u​dD\_{aud} as ADV\_REMARK or NA. This resulted 13698 sentences (2.37%) labeled as ADV\_REMARK and the rest as NA. * • Dataset D1D\_{1}: We used the trained adverse remark classification BERT model from [PAPV23] to predict labels of each sentence in Da​u​dD\_{aud}. This resulted in 33308 sentences (5.75%) labeled as ADV\_REMARK and the rest as NA. In an attempt to select the most diverse sentences as adverse remarks, we clustered these 33308 sentences and selected 1 representative sentence from each cluster; this yielded 1152 sentences. For clustering, we used a Fast Clustering algorithm from sentence-transformers python library[reimers-2019-sentence-bert]. Then we used the MISTRAL-7B-Instruct LLM to label these 1152 sentences in the dataset Da​u​dD\_{aud} as ADV\_REMARK or NA. The prompt given to the LLM for classification was as follows: Check whether the following sentence from a financial audit report is an adverse remark. Sentence:SS Answer as "Yes" or "No" followed by a short explanation. Answer: and if the output is Yes then we label it as ADV\_REMARK, else NA. * • Dataset Da​n​n(1)D\_{ann}^{(1)}: This dataset consists of 300 randomly selected sentences from Da​u​dD\_{aud}, each labelled manually as either ADV\_REMARK or NA. The annotation guidelines are explained below. * • Dataset Da​n​n(2)D\_{ann}^{(2)}: For obtaining unseen sentences to evaluate sentence classification models, we used audit reports from 2015, removed the sentences that did not have any verb, or had length less than 10 words or greater than 50 words and from the remaining sentences, randomly selected 500 sentences making sure they are sufficiently different from each other to avoid near-duplicates. We designed detailed annotation guidelines using which we manually labelled each sentence as either ADV\_REMARK or NA. A total of 82 sentences (16.4%) were labeled as ADV\_REMARK and the remaining 418 as NA. * • Dataset Ds​t​r(S​L)D\_{str}^{(SL)}: We do not have gold-standard ground truth about misinformation for any company for any year. So to enable evaluation of our methods on misinformation detection, we created a silver-standard dataset, where for each company and each year, the label indicates whether its FS for that year contains misinformation or not. The method to assign such a predicted silver label is as follows. Let AA denote the audit report for a company CC for year YY. We use MISTRAL-7B-Instruct to label each sentence in AA as ADV\_REMARK or NA. We also use our specially-trained T5 classifier (explained below) to label each sentence in AA as ADV\_REMARK or NA. If AA contains at least 2 sentences both of which are labeled as ADV\_REMARK by both MISTRAL and T5 classifier, then we assign the label MISINFO (contains misinformation) to the tuple (C,Y)(C,Y); else we assign it the label NA. We denote this dataset as DS​LD\_{SL}. Using this method, out of 11460 companies (over 5 years), 27272727 companies (19.80%) were labeled as containing misinformation. Year-wise distribution of the silver labels is given in Figure [3](https://arxiv.org/html/2607.17797v1#S3.F3 "Figure 3 ‣ 3 Datasets ‣ Financial Audit Assistance using Misinformation Detection and Explanation").

